  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A calorimeter contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> of water at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, in which&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> of water at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is added.&nbsp;The mixture is well stirred and the resulting temperature is found to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>35</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>. The thermal capacity of the calorimeter is</p>\r",
